{\pard\sb360\sa120\keepn\b Evidence map \u183? questions-before-priors\par}
{\pard\sa160 The contribution claim placed against the prior work the corpus reaches. The map informs the editor's signed judgment of what is new; it does not make it.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Run {\f1 20261029-041808-author-4909a9e} \u183? corpus {\f1 1709b72c5d12} \u183? 45 candidates \u183? keyword search of the venues switched on in venues.yaml, through OpenAlex, then the works most cited among the hits and among what the paper cites, kept when they are in those venues\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b C1 \u183? an answer: a new claim on a question already posed\par}
{\pard\li720\ri720\sa160\i Existing work explains learning and action once theories, vocabularies, or awareness advantages exist; this paper supplies the pre-Bayesian passage from represented-class failure to a question, a fallible new representation, formulation, and renewed Bayesian judgment. Its distinctive economic and epistemic results are that an agent can value a finer actionable distinction before representing its answers, and that evidence generating a truth-blind representation cannot be counted again as independent validation. Embedding that passage in strategic interaction provides a mechanism by which play exposes or conceals coarse languages and establishes the existence of absorbing configurations with heterogeneous terminal languages and payoffs. The contribution is therefore upstream: question histories and rational stopping points can generate strategic heterogeneity before priors over the resulting theories are available. The paper endogenizes the strategically consequential transition into a new hypothesis space while leaving the semantic act that produces it primitive.\par}
{\pard\sa160 The question it answers, as the paper states it ({\f1 paper/paper.md:L14-L15}): How an actor whose causal language has failed comes to possess a new one: the step before a theory, or at least a hypothesis space, exists.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Felin & Zenger (2009) (W2062762321), which the paper does not cite, poses the question in substance: where novel beliefs and theories come from when they extend beyond experience, the step before a theory is in hand; it answers verbally and states none of the claim's formal results (pricing a finer distinction before its answers are represented, no evidential reuse of the generating record, absorbing configurations with heterogeneous terminal languages). The awareness models (W2145868584, W2115460181) take the newly expanded space as given and model beliefs or attitudes around it, Gavetti & Levinthal (W2113812196) treats representation shifts without modeling how a failed one is replaced, and Ryall (2003) (W2152908192) gives persistent strategic differences within a fixed belief space, so none states the claim; hence a new answer to a question already posed. The call on poses_question for W2062762321 is the deciding one and is close: it does not frame the starting point as the failure of a causal language.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Agreement: 3/3\par}
